<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sydney</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sydney is the capital of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a> and the largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, on Port Jackson on the south-east coast. It grew from the penal settlement founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-cove">Sydney Cove</a> by the First Fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-phillip">Arthur Phillip</a> in January 1788, the first British colony in Australia. It became a major port and commercial centre and now has more than 5 million inhabitants.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sydney.jpg" alt="Sydney" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benh LIEU SONG (Flickr) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sydney_Opera_House_and_Harbour_Bridge_Dusk_(2)_2019-06-21.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sydney is a coastal basin, with the Tasman Sea to the east, the Blue Mountains to the west, the Hawkesbury River to the north and the Woronora Plateau to the south. The urban area surrounds Sydney Harbour and extends about 60 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> to the escarpment of the Blue Mountains. The Cumberland Plain, relatively flat, lies to the south and west of the harbour, and the Hornsby Plateau, cut by steep valleys, lies to the north. The flat southern areas were developed first, and the northern reaches became more heavily populated only after the Sydney Harbour Bridge was built. About seventy surf beaches lie along the coast.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first people in the area were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-australians">Aboriginal Australians</a>. Flaked pebbles in Western Sydney's gravel might indicate human presence 45,000 to 50,000 years ago, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radiocarbon-dating">radiocarbon dating</a> shows human activity from around 30,000 years ago. Before the British arrived there were 4,000 to 8,000 Aboriginal people in the region, living by fishing, hunting and gathering plants and shellfish. Greater Sydney covers the traditional lands of 28 known clans, among them the Gadigal, whose territory along the southern shore of Port Jackson includes the site of the first British settlement.</p><p>In 1788 Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-phillip">Arthur Phillip</a>, the first governor of New South Wales, named the cove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-cove">Sydney Cove</a> after Home Secretary Thomas Townshend, 1st Viscount Sydney; its Aboriginal name was Warrane. The population was under 1,000 in 1788 and almost tripled in ten years to 2,953 with convict transportation. The first five governors had near autocratic power. Governor Macquarie set ambitious standards for building, and the convict Francis Greenway, pardoned in 1818, designed the Hyde Park Barracks in 1819. Sydney was declared a city in 1842, when an elected municipal council was set up. New South Wales achieved responsible government in 1856, and in 1901, with the federation of the Australian colonies, Sydney became the capital of the state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Greater Sydney consists of 658 suburbs spread across 33 local government areas, and the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> recorded a population of 5,231,150. The Australian Museum, opened in 1827, remains <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>'s oldest natural history museum, and the Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a> stands on the site of the first Government House. The Sydney Opera House is World Heritage listed. Ku-ring-gai Chase National Park holds around 1,500 pieces of Aboriginal rock art, the largest cluster of Indigenous sites in Australia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sydney” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 10,000 live in Australia, mainly around <b>Sydney</b>, about 15% of the world total, and about 2,500 in Jordan.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mandaeism">Mandaeism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
